# Troubleshooting

This page covers common issues when running eUNC.

## eUNC Does Not Start

Check the following:

- The loader URL is correct.
- The executor supports the loading functions required by eUNC.
- The source modules can be retrieved.
- The downloaded source is not empty.
- The source compiles successfully.

## HTTP Errors

An HTTP error may indicate:

- An incorrect URL.
- A renamed or removed file.
- A temporary network problem.
- An unavailable GitHub resource.

Make sure the URL matches the current repository structure.

## Attempt to Call a Nil Value

An error such as:

\`\`\`
attempt to call a nil value
\`\`\`

usually means the code attempted to call something that was not a function.

Possible causes include:

- A missing module function.
- A function name mismatch.
- A module returning an unexpected value.
- An executor missing a required capability.
- An error in the benchmark itself.

Check the traceback and identify which function was called.

## Tests Fail on an Executor

A failed test does not automatically indicate that the executor lacks the capability.

Consider:

- Whether the executor implements the function.
- Whether the implementation behaves differently.
- Whether the test assumptions are correct.
- Whether the executor environment affects the behavior.

See [False Results](false-results.md).

## UNKNOWN Results

Unknown means the test did not produce a conclusive classification.

Investigate the test implementation and its execution environment.

## Emojis Are Missing

Some executor consoles may not display Unicode symbols correctly.

This is a console rendering issue and does not necessarily affect the test result.

## Report Cannot Be Saved

Report export requires:

- A compatible writefile function.
- A JSON encoder.
- Permission to write the requested file.

Check the executor's file-writing capabilities.

## Reporting an Issue

If you encounter a reproducible problem, open an issue:

https://github.com/EnhancedUNC/eUNC/issues

Include:

- eUNC version or commit.
- Executor name and version.
- Test name.
- Expected behavior.
- Actual behavior.
- Relevant error output.
- Whether the issue is reproducible.

Avoid including passwords, private information, or unrelated executor data.